WEBVTT

NOTE
Canonical written narration timed from the exact local narration render receipt.
Pronunciation-only substitutions are intentionally absent from caption text.

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.187
This chapter asks a specific question: To test whether cross-layer effects

2
00:00:05.187 --> 00:00:09.847
are authorized, the stack needs explicit identities for the principal,

3
00:00:09.847 --> 00:00:14.508
execution domain, operation, target, permission class, ceiling, grant lifecycle, delegation,

4
00:00:14.508 --> 00:00:15.440
and receipt.

5
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.757
The tempting shortcut is insufficient: Role labels, prompts,

6
00:00:19.757 --> 00:00:25.225
and ambient process identity do not by themselves distinguish read, transform,

7
00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:30.694
disclose, write, execute, and approve authority or enforce delegation, expiry, revocation,

8
00:00:30.694 --> 00:00:32.185
and cross-domain approval.

9
00:00:32.785 --> 00:00:38.383
The chapter's core claim is this: External-effect authority should be represented

10
00:00:38.383 --> 00:00:43.981
as a versioned, revocable tuple binding principal, execution domain, operation, target,

11
00:00:43.981 --> 00:00:49.579
permission class, scope, ceiling, grant state, delegation, expiry or revocation epoch,

12
00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:51.615
and receipt obligations; capability,

13
00:00:51.955 --> 00:00:57.388
context access, route quality, or ambient process power alone confers none

14
00:00:57.388 --> 00:01:02.821
of it. Define principals, authorities, ceilings, grants, revocations, and handoff contracts.

15
00:01:02.821 --> 00:01:05.785
Separate knowledge access from action authority.

16
00:01:06.125 --> 00:01:11.380
Represent missing authority as a detectable failure rather than implicit permission.

17
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:16.719
A concrete implementation trace makes the proposal testable.

18
00:01:16.719 --> 00:01:23.235
An authority-transition schema plus a unified authority-tuple lifecycle that binds execution-domain

19
00:01:23.235 --> 00:01:29.158
ownership, scope, budget, trace, replay, grant, revocation, cross-domain approval, receipts,

20
00:01:29.158 --> 00:01:30.935
and protocol-versus-hardware-root boundaries.

21
00:01:31.275 --> 00:01:36.616
Separate knowledge access from action authority. Represent missing authority as a

22
00:01:36.616 --> 00:01:39.530
detectable failure rather than implicit permission.

23
00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:45.501
Bind principal, execution domain, operation, target, permission class, scope, budget account,

24
00:01:45.501 --> 00:01:49.597
trace, replay identity, grant, policy version, revocation epoch,

25
00:01:49.597 --> 00:01:54.204
and expiry in one authority tuple. Throughout the trace,

26
00:01:54.204 --> 00:01:58.300
one invariant remains visible: Authority never expands silently.

27
00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:03.832
The design can still fail. Authority creep. Confused-deputy tool calls.

28
00:02:03.832 --> 00:02:09.257
Memory access treated as action approval. Authority identity forks across budget,

29
00:02:09.257 --> 00:02:11.230
trace, replay, or revocation.

30
00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:15.539
The correct response is to stop, narrow, quarantine, compensate,

31
00:02:15.539 --> 00:02:19.950
or retain an owned residual rather than narrate uncertainty away.

32
00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:25.596
This chapter remains Design rationale at argument support. Its contracts, examples,

33
00:02:25.596 --> 00:02:29.725
tests, or local artifacts establish only their encoded scope.

34
00:02:29.725 --> 00:02:34.771
The chapter names proof targets rather than pretending they are already

35
00:02:34.771 --> 00:02:35.230
closed.

36
00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:40.489
Every accepted issuance, dispatch, and effect in the finite reachable grant

37
00:02:40.489 --> 00:02:45.409
model remains within the caller ceiling and exactly binds grant ID,

38
00:02:45.409 --> 00:02:49.433
principal, operation, target, epoch, expiry, remaining uses, approval, dispatch,

39
00:02:49.433 --> 00:02:50.775
and effect custody.

40
00:02:51.115 --> 00:02:57.625
The reachable model and independent consumer reject authority widening, confused-deputy substitution,

41
00:02:57.625 --> 00:03:02.952
stale or expired grants, missing approval/dispatch/effect receipts, post-revocation dispatch,

42
00:03:02.952 --> 00:03:07.095
effect without dispatch, and consumed one-shot reuse.

43
00:03:07.695 --> 00:03:12.659
No chapter claim or evidence state is promoted by this visual.

44
00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:17.623
A named mechanism is not proof of correct implementation or useful

45
00:03:17.623 --> 00:03:22.587
deployment. A local check or formal model does not establish real-world

46
00:03:22.587 --> 00:03:27.100
enforcement. No safety, transfer, state-of-the-art, AGI, or ASI conclusion follows.

47
00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.915
These boundaries matter because an explanatory derivative is useful only if

48
00:03:31.915 --> 00:03:35.170
it preserves the evidence ceiling of its source.

49
00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:41.005
At most, this visual explains the chapter's design rationale at argument

50
00:03:41.005 --> 00:03:45.764
support; it adds no empirical, deployment, safety, transfer, state-of-the-art, AGI,

51
00:03:45.764 --> 00:03:50.999
or ASI result. The next chapter is Failure Modes of Ungoverned

52
00:03:50.999 --> 00:03:51.475
Intelligence.

53
00:03:51.815 --> 00:03:57.476
It takes responsibility for Governance, reliability, and self-improvement claims cannot be

54
00:03:57.476 --> 00:04:02.622
tested unless their failure conditions, observable boundary events, evidence records,

55
00:04:02.622 --> 00:04:05.195
and owners are declared prospectively.
